1
00:00:00,720 --> 00:00:02,707
Hey everyone. To understand and solve ...

2
00:00:02,707 --> 00:00:05,439
business problems, we need to start with the basics.

3
00:00:05,840 --> 00:00:07,360
People respond to incentives.

4
00:00:07,680 --> 00:00:12,080
But when outcomes fall short, it's not because people are irrational.

5
00:00:12,080 --> 00:00:15,120
It's because the system is this term.

6
00:00:15,120 --> 00:00:17,840
You'll gather tools to diagnose these kind of problems.

7
00:00:18,240 --> 00:00:21,760
We'll keep coming back to three key questions.

8
00:00:23,040 --> 00:00:24,480
Who is making the bad decision?

9
00:00:24,720 --> 00:00:27,040
Do they have enough information to make a good one?

10
00:00:27,520 --> 00:00:30,880
And is there an incentive to make a good decision?

11
00:00:31,920 --> 00:00:34,880
By the end of this course, these questions will become second nature.

12
00:00:35,920 --> 00:00:39,760
Let's look at a classic problem of misaligned incentives.

13
00:00:39,920 --> 00:00:42,320
On one side, leadership has clear goals.

14
00:00:42,320 --> 00:00:45,200
They want to have growth, efficiency and better margins.

15
00:00:45,600 --> 00:00:47,943
On the other, the frontline employees feel ...

16
00:00:47,943 --> 00:00:49,244
confused, disengaged,

17
00:00:49,244 --> 00:00:51,262
or worse, they're working hard in the ...

18
00:00:51,262 --> 00:00:52,238
wrong direction.

19
00:00:52,880 --> 00:00:55,280
What we see here isn't laziness or apathy.

20
00:00:55,280 --> 00:00:56,720
It's a failure of the system design.

21
00:00:57,280 --> 00:01:00,760
Our job is to ask who's making the bad decision and why?

22
00:01:00,760 --> 00:01:02,080
Does that make sense to them?

23
00:01:03,440 --> 00:01:05,520
Enter the rational actor paradigm.

24
00:01:05,680 --> 00:01:09,920
It says people act rationally, optimally and self interestly.

25
00:01:10,720 --> 00:01:13,543
They have certain incentives in place for them and they ...

26
00:01:13,543 --> 00:01:15,199
want to act on those incentives.

27
00:01:15,680 --> 00:01:18,463
So if we have someone making a bad decision from ...

28
00:01:18,463 --> 00:01:20,390
your point of view, it probably,

29
00:01:20,390 --> 00:01:22,959
probably makes perfect sense from theirs.

30
00:01:23,200 --> 00:01:25,520
Which leads us to our next two questions.

31
00:01:25,920 --> 00:01:29,280
Do they have the right information and do they have ...

32
00:01:29,280 --> 00:01:30,640
a reason to act on it?

33
00:01:30,720 --> 00:01:32,360
If we need to change their behavior,

34
00:01:32,360 --> 00:01:34,000
we have to change their incentives.

35
00:01:35,280 --> 00:01:37,450
Which brings us to incentives,

36
00:01:37,450 --> 00:01:39,119
behavior and outcomes.

37
00:01:39,440 --> 00:01:41,760
This is a loop we'll diagnose over and over.

38
00:01:41,760 --> 00:01:44,664
When outcomes are off, it's usually because there's a ...

39
00:01:44,664 --> 00:01:47,439
break or a distortion somewhere else in this chain.

40
00:01:49,320 --> 00:01:51,594
Are the right incentives in place for your employees to ...

41
00:01:51,594 --> 00:01:52,879
produce the outcomes you want?

42
00:01:52,880 --> 00:01:56,120
Think of a salesperson paid on volume and not profit.

43
00:01:56,280 --> 00:01:59,240
They'll sell more, but the company might earn less.

44
00:01:59,880 --> 00:02:02,520
Behavior follow an incentive, not the intention.

45
00:02:03,560 --> 00:02:05,720
Now let's layer in marginal analysis.

46
00:02:05,720 --> 00:02:08,840
This tool helps us ask what's the next best action?

47
00:02:08,920 --> 00:02:10,680
Does it pay off for the individual?

48
00:02:11,000 --> 00:02:13,480
Compare the marginal benefits to the marginal cost.

49
00:02:14,080 --> 00:02:16,259
If the marginal cost of doing something outweighs the ...

50
00:02:16,259 --> 00:02:18,099
marginal benefit, we shouldn't be surprised ...

51
00:02:18,099 --> 00:02:19,358
when people choose otherwise.

52
00:02:19,680 --> 00:02:23,680
Is working one extra hour the marginal cost worth the pay?

53
00:02:23,680 --> 00:02:25,120
The marginal benefit.

54
00:02:25,680 --> 00:02:28,909
Here we know that the rational actor will continue an ...

55
00:02:28,909 --> 00:02:32,505
activity up to the point where the marginal cost equals the ...

56
00:02:32,505 --> 00:02:33,679
marginal benefit.

57
00:02:33,840 --> 00:02:35,826
Beyond that, the rational actor will not ...

58
00:02:35,826 --> 00:02:37,519
continue to pursue that activity.

59
00:02:38,880 --> 00:02:41,526
This tool is how we zoom in on decision points and ...

60
00:02:41,526 --> 00:02:44,239
assess whether incentives line up at the margin.

61
00:02:45,520 --> 00:02:48,644
In a well designed system, personal gains fit perfectly ...

62
00:02:48,644 --> 00:02:50,239
with organizational goals.

63
00:02:50,240 --> 00:02:51,760
That's an incentive alignment.

64
00:02:52,000 --> 00:02:54,700
Here we have personal goals and organizational goals not ...

65
00:02:54,700 --> 00:02:55,600
fitting together.

66
00:02:55,840 --> 00:02:57,320
We need them to fit together.

67
00:02:57,320 --> 00:02:59,741
If we place the proper incentives in place,

68
00:02:59,741 --> 00:03:01,759
then we can align the puzzle pieces.

69
00:03:02,320 --> 00:03:04,520
Then everyone's doing what's best for them.

70
00:03:04,520 --> 00:03:06,621
And it's also best for the team,

71
00:03:06,621 --> 00:03:08,399
the firm and the customer.

72
00:03:08,880 --> 00:03:11,640
Good alignment doesn't require micromanagement.

73
00:03:11,640 --> 00:03:13,440
It requires structural clarity.

74
00:03:14,000 --> 00:03:16,000
But alignment isn't static.

75
00:03:16,480 --> 00:03:17,520
Businesses can.

76
00:03:17,520 --> 00:03:18,800
Business conditions change.

77
00:03:18,800 --> 00:03:20,560
People respond to what's rewarded.

78
00:03:20,800 --> 00:03:22,680
That's why we build feedback loops.

79
00:03:22,680 --> 00:03:25,093
We measure the outcomes, we evaluate the gap and ...

80
00:03:25,093 --> 00:03:26,239
tweak the incentives.

81
00:03:26,560 --> 00:03:29,200
These loops keep the systems honest and nimble.

82
00:03:30,640 --> 00:03:33,859
When incentives are aligned and feedback is tight,

83
00:03:33,859 --> 00:03:36,479
teams pull in unison toward shared goals.

84
00:03:37,200 --> 00:03:38,840
Diversity of perspective adds strength.

85
00:03:38,840 --> 00:03:40,720
Shared direction gives it focus.

86
00:03:41,280 --> 00:03:45,440
And it all comes back to our three who's making the bad decision?

87
00:03:45,600 --> 00:03:47,680
Do they have enough information to make a good one?

88
00:03:47,680 --> 00:03:50,080
And is there an incentive to make a good decision?

89
00:03:51,440 --> 00:03:54,320
We'll revisit these ideas again and again throughout the term.

90
00:03:54,320 --> 00:03:56,040
Each case, each tool and each ...

91
00:03:56,040 --> 00:03:59,408
framework will give us more clarity and control over the ...

92
00:03:59,408 --> 00:04:01,199
messy, high stakes problems.

93
00:04:01,440 --> 00:04:03,285
By the end, we'll have a mindset and ...

94
00:04:03,285 --> 00:04:04,239
methods to solve.

95
00:04:05,440 --> 00:04:06,440
All right, that's it.

96
00:04:06,440 --> 00:04:07,600
Hope you've enjoyed it.
